<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nine Years' War (Ireland)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1593">1593</a>–<a href="/years/1603">1603</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Tyrone's Rebellion · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nine Years' War was a rebellion of Gaelic Irish lords against English rule, fought from 1593 to 1603. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-oneill-earl-of-tyrone">Hugh O'Neill, Earl of Tyrone</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-hugh-odonnell">Red Hugh O'Donnell</a>, with Maguire of Fermanagh and other Ulster lords, it won victories such as the Yellow Ford in 1598. A Spanish force landed at Kinsale in 1601, but defeat there led to O'Neill's surrender in 1603, the Flight of the Earls in 1607 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland.jpg" alt="Nine Years' War (Ireland)" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacobfrid · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nine_Years_War_January1600.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Kingdom of Ireland was established as an English client state in 1542, and by the early 1590s the English state's control had spread from the Pale over much of the island. The execution of Gaelic lords, the pillaging of settlements by appointed sheriffs and the persecution of Catholics bred resentment among the Gaelic nobility. In 1591 the Lord Deputy Fitzwilliam had The MacMahon hanged and his lordship in Monaghan divided for resisting an English sheriff. Hugh O'Neill, granted the earldom of Tyrone in 1585, could arm and feed over 8,000 men with Spanish aid, and from 1591 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-hugh-odonnell">Hugh Roe O'Donnell</a> had been in contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The war is generally dated from May 1593, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-maguire-lord-of-fermanagh">Hugh Maguire</a> resisted the appointment of Humphrey Willis as sheriff of Fermanagh. O'Neill at first helped the English, hoping to be made Lord President of Ulster, but when the post was marked for his enemy Henry Bagenal he joined the rising openly in February 1595 with an attack on the Blackwater Fort. After a confederate victory at the Battle of Clontibret, up to 2,000 English troops were killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-yellow-ford">Battle of the Yellow Ford</a> in 1598, among them Bagenal, the heaviest English defeat in Ireland to that time. Risings followed across the country, and in Munster as many as 9,000 men rebelled.</p><p>Robert Devereux, 2nd Earl of Essex, arrived in 1599 with over 17,000 troops but achieved little. His successor, Lord Mountjoy, landed forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Derry</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrickfergus">Carrickfergus</a>, whose commanders devastated the countryside to bring famine and killed civilians at random. After the rout at Kinsale on 5/6 January 1602, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-hugh-odonnell">Hugh Roe O'Donnell</a> left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to plead for another landing and died of a fever in 1602. O'Neill held out in the forests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tir-eoghain">Tír Eoghain</a> until 30 March 1603, when he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-mellifont">Treaty of Mellifont</a>; the last holdout, West Breifne, fell on 25 April 1603.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>James I granted the Ulster chiefs pardons and their estates on condition that they give up their Irish titles and private armies, for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> could no longer afford the war. Irish sources claimed that as many as 60,000 people died in the Ulster famine of 1602–03 alone, and at least 30,000 English soldiers died in Ireland, mainly of disease; the total was certainly at least 100,000. It was the largest and costliest conflict England fought in the Elizabethan era. After the lords left in 1607, their lands were confiscated in 1608.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nine Years' War (Ireland)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It is said to commemorate the assault of 700 British grenadiers on the French-held fortress of Namur in August 1695, during the <b>Nine Years' War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-british-grenadiers">The British Grenadiers</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Nine Years' War</b>, a Gaelic Irish army under Hugh O'Neill ambushed 1,750 English troops under Henry Bagenal near Clontibret on their way to relieve Monaghan Castle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-27">27 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Hugh Maguire fought with Hugh O'Neill in the <b>Nine Years' War</b> and died in 1600.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fermanagh">Kingdom of Fermanagh</a></li><li><blockquote>Red Hugh O'Donnell joined Hugh O'Neill in the <b>Nine Years' War</b> against England.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tir-chonaill">Kingdom of Tir Chonaill</a></li><li><blockquote>Hugh O'Neill led the <b>Nine Years' War</b> against England, but defeat at Kinsale in 1601 forced his submission.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tir-eoghain">Kingdom of Tir Eoghain</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Brian Og fought with O'Neill in the <b>Nine Years' War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-breifne">West Breifne</a></li></ul></section>
